  explanation:'**Jude prints the same five options in the same order as `pedhd-neuro-8`, and his discriminating token is the age, the morning myoclonic jerk and the sleep-deprivation trigger.**\n\nThe syndrome table places **juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)** at **10-20 years** with **"myoclonic jerks in the first hour after waking — classically throwing drinks or cereal about in the morning; absences often precede, from the end of the first decade; GTCS on awakening"** (`neurological` neuro-10). Jude is 14, already known to have both generalised tonic-clonic AND absence seizures (matching "absences often precede" the myoclonic jerks), and now describes exactly the deck\'s own signature complaint — spilling his morning tea from an involuntary arm jerk, the textbook restatement of "throwing drinks or cereal about in the morning". Sleep deprivation as a specific aggravating factor for JME myoclonic jerks is a well-recognised trigger consistent with "worse if he has stayed up late the night before", and the deck notes JME is **"often not recognised by referring clinicians"** (neuro-10) — precisely the kind of easily-missed morning jerk Jude is now flagging.\n\n**Why the other four are each wrong, following the reasoning set out in `pedhd-neuro-8`:** childhood Rolandic epilepsy causes nocturnal focal seizures with facial/oral involvement, not morning myoclonic arm jerks; childhood absence epilepsy alone would not explain a new myoclonic jerk or the tonic-clonic seizures already established; Lennox-Gastaut and infantile spasms both onset far earlier in childhood, with multiple severe seizure types and, in West syndrome, developmental regression — none of which fits a 14-year-old doing relatively well at school.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
